# The sign-in and password-reset flow, end to end.
#
# Open in VS Code with the REST Client extension (humao.rest-client) or in the
# JetBrains HTTP client; both read this format. Send requests with the "Send
# Request" link that appears above each one.
#
# ── There is no "create an account" request in here ──────────────────────────
# Not an omission. The API has no route that creates a user, on purpose: an
# unauthenticated one would let anybody into the newsroom, and an authenticated
# one still puts account creation behind whoever happens to hold a token.
# Accounts are made by a person at a terminal, with the database in front of
# them:
#
#   cd backend
#   npm run account -- add --email someone@example.com --name "Their Name" --role writer
#   npm run account -- add --email someone@example.com --name "Their Name" --role dev
#   npm run account -- list
#
# `add` creates the account with no password at all and prints a single-use
# setup link. The person whose account it is chooses the password in a browser,
# and nobody else ever holds it. That link is the same mechanism as "I forgot my
# password" — which is why the flow below is worth running: it is exercised on
# every new account, not only on the rare occasion somebody forgets.
#
# `npm run account -- add` builds its link from APP_URL, so run it with APP_URL
# set to the environment you mean:
#
#   APP_URL=https://vibe-vic.vercel.app npm run account -- add --email … --role dev
#
# ── The two accounts this file assumes ───────────────────────────────────────
#   eddymax3715@gmail.com    DEV     — Eddy Kilonzo
#   vickiplimo901@gmail.com  WRITER  — Victor Kiplimo
#
# ── The one rule that fails requests silently if you miss it ─────────────────
# Passwords are a minimum of TWELVE characters (ResetPasswordDto, LoginDto). A
# shorter one comes back 400 from validation, before anything is hashed. There
# is no character-class rule — a long ordinary phrase is the intended shape.

@api = https://vibevic.onrender.com/api
# @api = http://localhost:4000/api

@dev = eddymax3715@gmail.com
@writer = vickiplimo901@gmail.com


### 1 · Is this API reachable at all
# No auth, no origin, nothing clever. On Render's free tier the first request
# after an idle period takes 30-60s while the instance wakes; that is the
# expected slow one, not a failure.
GET {{api}}/genres


### 2 · Ask for a reset link — the DEV account
# Always 202, whatever happens next: the server is saying it accepted the
# request, not that an email went out. That is deliberate, and it is why this
# request cannot tell you whether an address has an account.
#
# What a 202 does NOT rule out:
#   · no account for the address        → nothing sent, logged as such
#   · the throttle already spent        → 3 requests per address per 15 minutes
#
# A 503 here is the one visible failure, and it means the server has no mailer:
# SMTP_HOST, SMTP_USER, SMTP_PASS, SMTP_FROM and APP_URL are what it wants.
POST {{api}}/auth/forgot-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{dev}}"
}


### 3 · Ask for a reset link — the WRITER account
POST {{api}}/auth/forgot-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{writer}}"
}


### 4 · Spend the link and set a password
# Take the token from the `?token=` in the emailed URL — 64 hex characters. It
# is posted in the body rather than left in the query string it arrived on,
# because a URL ends up in browser history, in a Referer header and in every
# proxy log in between.
#
# One outstanding link per account: asking again in step 2 kills the previous
# token immediately, so use the newest email. Setting the password ends every
# session the account currently holds.
#
# Twelve characters minimum. Replace the placeholder below — it is a shape, not
# a suggestion.
POST {{api}}/auth/reset-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "token": "PASTE-THE-64-HEX-CHARACTERS-FROM-THE-EMAIL-LINK-HERE-000000000000",
  "password": "choose a long ordinary phrase"
}


### 5 · Sign in
# Every failure is one 401 with the same body, whether the address is unknown or
# the password is wrong. Eight attempts per address per 10 minutes.
# Copy `token` out of the response for step 6.
POST {{api}}/auth/token
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{dev}}",
  "password": "the password set in step 4"
}


### 6 · What the server believes about the caller
# Confirms the token works and shows the scopes the role carries — this is where
# the DEV/WRITER split becomes visible.
@token = PASTE-THE-TOKEN-FROM-STEP-5

GET {{api}}/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 7 · The preflight the deployed site actually sends
# Not part of the flow — a check on configuration. A browser on the deployed
# frontend sends this before any POST, and it must come back with an
# `access-control-allow-origin` naming the origin. If that header is absent the
# request is 204 and still blocked, and every call from the site fails while
# curl without an Origin keeps answering 200.
#
# The value in CORS_ORIGINS must have no trailing slash: an `Origin` header is a
# scheme, host and port and nothing else.
OPTIONS {{api}}/auth/forgot-password
Origin: https://vibe-vic.vercel.app
Access-Control-Request-Method: POST
Access-Control-Request-Headers: content-type
